1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,081
It is just before midnight on Saturday the
first of September, 1666.

2
00:00:04,081 --> 00:00:08,161
In a narrow timber-framed house
on Pudding Lane,

3
00:00:08,161 --> 00:00:12,242
a young woman in her late teens or early twenties is
finishing her work in a bakery kitchen.

4
00:00:12,242 --> 00:00:16,323
We do not know her name. No record of
it has ever been found.

5
00:00:16,323 --> 00:00:20,403
In two hours she will become the first person to
die in the Great Fire of London.

6
00:00:20,403 --> 00:00:24,484
She will die because she will not
jump from the window.

7
00:00:24,484 --> 00:00:28,565
Everything you are about to see has been recreated from the household accounts, the British
Library letter of October 1666, and the parliamentary investigation that followed.

8
00:00:28,565 --> 00:00:32,645
The bakery belongs to a man named
Thomas Farriner.

9
00:00:32,645 --> 00:00:36,726
He bakes ship's biscuit for
the Royal Navy.

10
00:00:36,726 --> 00:00:40,807
His shop sits on the eastern side of Pudding Lane — a narrow medieval
street that runs from Eastcheap down toward the river,

11
00:00:40,807 --> 00:00:44,887
named for the carts of offal that pass through
it on their way to the Thames.

12
00:00:44,887 --> 00:00:48,968
The kitchen at midnight smells of
cooling oak ash,

13
00:00:48,968 --> 00:00:53,049
of yeast left rising for
tomorrow's loaves,

14
00:00:53,049 --> 00:00:57,129
and faintly of the river — three streets south,
low and slow in the dry summer.

15
00:00:57,129 --> 00:01:01,210
The flagstones are warm from a
day of baking.

16
00:01:01,210 --> 00:01:05,291
The brick of the oven is still
hot to the touch.

17
00:01:05,291 --> 00:01:09,371
A copper pot ticks softly as it cools
on the trestle table.

18
00:01:09,371 --> 00:01:13,452
She would have heard the watchmen calling midnight — past
twelve, and all is well — somewhere up Eastcheap.

19
00:01:13,452 --> 00:01:17,533
She would have heard the timber of the house
above her settling into the night.

20
00:01:17,533 --> 00:01:21,613
She would have heard the rats
in the walls.

21
00:01:21,613 --> 00:01:25,694
She would have heard the oven, fifteen feet across the kitchen from
where she stood, still breathing its heat into the room.

22
00:01:25,694 --> 00:01:29,775
London in 1666 is a city of timber.

23
00:01:29,775 --> 00:01:33,855
Most of its houses were built in the time of
Elizabeth, almost a century before —

24
00:01:33,855 --> 00:01:37,936
oak beams, lath and plaster walls, tarred wood eaves that overhang the lanes
until the upper storeys nearly touch the houses opposite.

25
00:01:37,936 --> 00:01:42,017
Three storeys, sometimes four. A street like Pudding Lane is ten feet
wide at the cobbles and three feet wide at the eaves.

26
00:01:42,017 --> 00:01:46,097
The summer of 1666 has been the
driest in living memory.

27
00:01:46,097 --> 00:01:50,178
The Thames is low. The wells are thin. The oak
of the city is dry to the heart.

28
00:01:50,178 --> 00:01:54,258
For four days an east wind has been
blowing across the rooftops.

29
00:01:54,258 --> 00:01:58,339
At ten o'clock that Saturday night, Thomas Farriner went down into the
bakehouse to rake the coals in the oven for the night.

30
00:01:58,339 --> 00:02:02,420
He raked them carefully.

31
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:06,500
He swept the embers toward the centre of the
firebox, away from the walls.

32
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:10,581
He closed the iron door. He believed
the fire was out.

33
00:02:10,581 --> 00:02:14,662
He went up the narrow wooden
stairs to bed.

34
00:02:14,662 --> 00:02:18,742
Somewhere in the bakehouse — perhaps lodged
in the soot of the chimney,

35
00:02:18,742 --> 00:02:22,823
perhaps fallen into the kindling
stacked beside the wall,

36
00:02:22,823 --> 00:02:26,904
perhaps caught in the seam of an oak floorboard —
a single ember was still alive.

37
00:02:26,904 --> 00:02:30,984
For an hour, nothing happens.

38
00:02:30,984 --> 00:02:35,065
For another hour, still nothing.

39
00:02:35,065 --> 00:02:39,146
The house above is asleep — Farriner
in his bedchamber,

40
00:02:39,146 --> 00:02:43,226
Hanna in her room across the landing,

41
00:02:43,226 --> 00:02:47,307
Dagger in a smaller room toward the back,

42
00:02:47,307 --> 00:02:51,388
the maid in a room beneath the eaves
on the upper floor.

43
00:02:51,388 --> 00:02:55,468
The ember is still alive.

44
00:02:55,468 --> 00:02:59,549
At about a quarter past midnight, the kindling
stacked beside the oven begins to smoke.

45
00:02:59,549 --> 00:03:03,630
By half past midnight, the
kindling is burning.

46
00:03:03,630 --> 00:03:07,710
By one o'clock, the fire has climbed into the
wooden wall behind the oven.

47
00:03:07,710 --> 00:03:11,791
The oak beams are dry to the heart.
They catch quickly.

48
00:03:11,791 --> 00:03:15,872
Smoke begins to rise through the
floorboards of the rooms above.

49
00:03:15,872 --> 00:03:19,952
In a small room near the back of the house, Thomas Dagger — the journeyman
who works alongside Farriner — wakes choking on the smoke.

50
00:03:19,952 --> 00:03:24,033
He shouts for the others.

51
00:03:24,033 --> 00:03:28,114
Farriner wakes. His daughter Hanna
wakes. The maid wakes.

52
00:03:28,114 --> 00:03:32,194
The bakehouse below them is fully engulfed. The wooden stairs are
burning. The fire has been moving while they slept.

53
00:03:32,194 --> 00:03:36,275
There is one way out — the window of an upstairs bedroom that looks across a
narrow gap of perhaps four feet to the gutter of the next house.

54
00:03:36,275 --> 00:03:40,356
It is a drop of ten feet to the cobbles of Pudding Lane below, or
a climb across the gap to the neighbour's roof.

55
00:03:40,356 --> 00:03:44,436
Thomas Farriner goes out first. He swings his legs through the
casement, finds the gutter with his foot, and steps across.

56
00:03:44,436 --> 00:03:48,517
Hanna follows. She is in her shift. She finds
her father's hand and crosses.

57
00:03:48,517 --> 00:03:52,598
Dagger climbs out after them, lowers himself onto the gutter, and follows
them down through the neighbour's house and into the lane.

58
00:03:52,598 --> 00:03:56,678
The maid stays at the window.

59
00:03:56,678 --> 00:04:00,759
We do not know what she said. We do not
know what they said to her.

60
00:04:00,759 --> 00:04:04,840
The letter that records the night, written that October to a Member of
Parliament, says only this: his mayd was burnt in ye hous,

61
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:08,920
not adventuring to escape as he, his daughter, and his
man did, out of ye windowe and gutter.

62
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:13,001
She did not adventure to escape.

63
00:04:13,001 --> 00:04:17,082
She was afraid of the jump.

64
00:04:17,082 --> 00:04:21,162
The roof of the bakery collapses
within minutes.

65
00:04:21,162 --> 00:04:25,243
The fire spreads to the Star
Inn next door,

66
00:04:25,243 --> 00:04:29,324
then to the houses across Fish
Street Hill,

67
00:04:29,324 --> 00:04:33,404
then to the church of St Magnus the
Martyr by the river.

68
00:04:33,404 --> 00:04:37,485
By dawn on Sunday, three hundred
houses are gone.

69
00:04:37,485 --> 00:04:41,566
By Sunday evening, the fire has
crossed Cannon Street.

70
00:04:41,566 --> 00:04:45,646
By Wednesday morning, four days later, St Paul's
Cathedral has collapsed in on itself.

71
00:04:45,646 --> 00:04:49,727
The molten lead of its roof has run in rivers
down Ludgate Hill into the Thames.

72
00:04:49,727 --> 00:04:53,808
Four-fifths of the City of London is gone.

73
00:04:53,808 --> 00:04:57,888
When the fire finally dies, thirteen thousand two
hundred houses have been destroyed.

74
00:04:57,888 --> 00:05:01,969
Eighty-seven parish churches.

75
00:05:01,969 --> 00:05:06,050
The Royal Exchange. Old St
Paul's. The Guildhall.

76
00:05:06,050 --> 00:05:10,130
About a hundred thousand
Londoners are homeless.

77
00:05:10,130 --> 00:05:14,211
The official death toll is six.

78
00:05:14,211 --> 00:05:18,291
The real number was almost
certainly much higher.

79
00:05:18,291 --> 00:05:22,372
Many of the urban poor — the elderly, the sick, the very young, the
servants in upstairs rooms — were never accounted for.

80
00:05:22,372 --> 00:05:26,453
Their bodies, where they were found at all,
had been cremated in the heat.

81
00:05:26,453 --> 00:05:30,533
The maid in the bakery is counted
as one of the six.

82
00:05:30,533 --> 00:05:34,614
Her name was never recorded.

83
00:05:34,614 --> 00:05:38,695
Thomas Farriner survived.

84
00:05:38,695 --> 00:05:42,775
He rebuilt his bakery on the same spot on
Pudding Lane within two years.

85
00:05:42,775 --> 00:05:46,856
He never publicly accepted that the fire
had started in his oven.

86
00:05:46,856 --> 00:05:50,937
He died in December 1670, four years and three months after the night
she did not jump, still maintaining his innocence.

87
00:05:50,937 --> 00:05:55,017
Two hundred and two feet from where the bakery stood, the
city of London built a column to the fire.

88
00:05:55,017 --> 00:05:59,098
It is two hundred and two feet
tall. It still stands.

89
00:05:59,098 --> 00:06:03,179
The column has a long Latin
inscription at its base.

90
00:06:03,179 --> 00:06:07,259
It names the kings who reigned during the fire and
during the column's construction.

91
00:06:07,259 --> 00:06:11,340
It names the architects, Christopher
Wren and Robert Hooke.

92
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:15,421
It names the Lord Mayor and the city
officials who commissioned it.

93
00:06:15,421 --> 00:06:19,501
It does not name the woman who died first.

94
00:06:19,501 --> 00:06:23,582
Hers was never written down.
